const std::array<AdultBook,originalAdultCount>& adultBooks(){
    static constexpr std::array<AdultBook,originalAdultCount> collection{{
        {"The Signal Shift","Fiction","An observer should decide if a sign deserves another listening night.","#62383b",{{
            {"The anomaly",
             "At two and seventeen, the radio telescope graph drew a line that did not belong to the interference inventory. Elena stopped playback and expanded the last few seconds. At the observatory's shed only the equipment's fan and a branch's pounding were heard against the wall. The line was narrow, oblique and fleeting. He had been shown up at the time his antenna was passing through an empty area of his program.\nHe put his hour on paper. The automatic alerts retained the record but wrote it forced her to look at the clock twice.",
             "Before we notify his supervisor, Elena asked herself a more modest question. Can I have a look again if I look again? He requested a second pass and also noted his antenna's position. The search engine found no matching at the week's file. That's what she told her. The file was made up of incomplete nights, different equipment and shifts where someone had lowered the threshold to recover weak data.\nElena added a column to the notebook and entitled it \"conditions.\" Below was the gain, temperature and status of the network. If I had to remember the night after, those details would matter as much as the line."},
            {"The second antenna",
             "The second passing ended without a single figure. Elena called Omar, attending a small station across the saw. He asked her to check the same seconds and avoid describing what form she was looking for. He wanted coincidence, if it existed, to survive two different ways of looking. While waiting, she copied the coordinates of a nearby field and checked that the team recognized a source that they both regularly observed.\nOmar called back 20 minutes later. He had also seen a disturbance. His hour coincided, but his antenna was signalling another region.",
             "For a while they discussed the hour. One registry used the recipient's time and the other that of the station's computer, so they compared them with a common reference. The difference was reduced to less than a second. The data was with a source that affected both installations, but there could still be a ground explanation. Elena wrote down the comparison without giving her a name.\nThey later requested the maintenance schedule and information about the area's transmission. They didn't need a brilliant explanation. They needed one they could test without deleting what they had seen."},
            {"The Blind Test",
             "The following day, the supervisor suggested showing the graph at the weekly meeting. Elena prepared a number of cuts: the original line, several known interferences and fragments with nothing outstanding. He tipped his order and delivered numbered copies. Half the team chose the right cut. The other half noted similar phenomena at the controls. That result changed the conversation: the pattern that seemed unique at two o'clock in the morning was similar to others as nobody knew where to look.\nAt the end of the meeting, Elena put two charts together and wrote the date of each source under.",
             "Omar found his first useful track in a maintenance folder. At that time a temporary link had been activated at a loin with a view to both stations. They asked the operator to light it up again while antennas differed between several points. The second essay saw his line. The form was slightly changing with the recipient's adjustment and from both locations they were able to repeat the result.\nDuring the trial, Elena did not feel that she lost a good story. He was pleased that the second night would be useful to distinguish an explanation from another and that the procedure would be registered."},
            {"The report",
             "For his report, he separated his comments from his account about them. He retained his hours, his adjustments, his second passing's failed response and his link tests. They left a copy of the data as they had come and another with the corrections. They also explain how to rebuild the charts so that the next shift could check the result without depending on their memory.\nThe sign remained in the interference catalogue with a note about sensitive recipients. A month later, someone spotted her in less than ten minutes.",
             "Last afternoon of summer, Elena returned to the shed and found a new student reading the report. He had drawn a circle by his condition column. I wanted to know what I had to do if, at his first shift, I saw something that nobody had put up earlier. Elena opened the drawer and put an empty notebook and put it next to the keyboard.\nHe shown him where to check his watch and how to prepare a second observation. Then they went out and looked at the antenna, and they're still up against the sky. The student asked what name they would put to the first register. Elena responded that they could talk about that at dawn."}
        }}},
        {"The City That Breathes","Engineering","A field notebook about air, water and decisions in an orbital habitat.","#354f54",{{
            {"The balance sheet",
             "At the simulator, the habitat looks like a miniature town. It had deposits, ducts, work zones and a board where every variable could be followed during the shift. Lucía explained with a notebook: on one page she wrote what entered each system and on the next what came out. The comparison gave a first clue as the air differed from what was intended. The absolute quantity and how long they remained imported.\nThe method was simple as all the meters worked. The practice began as some stopped matching with others.",
             "At the first test, the simulator indicated that carbon dioxide increased in a busy room. Lucia asked his partner to check where his sensor was before attributing it to the whole module. The air circulation could get more from one point to another. They also wished to know if the increase coincided with the exercise of the crew or with a change in flow.\nThey had two graphs, one from the team and another from jobs. When they put them together, the most pronounced slope began several minutes before anyone entered."},
            {"Valve",
             "The second round left the room empty. Lucia prepared a flowtable and repeated the same settings one by one. He saw that the difference came about as the system changed its circuit. A fan continued to turn normally, but some of the air followed a different path from that shown by the board. The team propose to measure directly at two accessible points of the model.\nIt was an uncomfortable contrast for those who had built the screen. That was an opportunity as well. If the model had a plausible defect, they could record how it manifold before relying on it for another practice.",
             "The valve was listed as open because the controller had sent the order. The reading had no confirmation of the movement. The group added two lines to the log, one for the order and another for the observed response. That distinction allowed them to detect another malfunction that had been unnoticed.\nIn new practice, one person operated the system and another person marked when they had checked each change. Instead of always repeating the same series, they could test variations and figure out what measurements were sufficient to distinguish them. It soon became clear what needed redundancy and what only required a simple test."},
            {"Water and priorities",
             "The water had its own accounting. At exercise, one party came out with wet air, another regained and another remained at the warehouses. A small leak and misestimate of moisture could draw similar curves. Lucia requested a separate measurement of water collection and compared the result with the fall of the tank. The temperature changes were noted at the same time.\nThe group estimated how long the reserve would last in different scenarios. They could have decided when to repeat a measurement and how long they had to understand the cause before changing the plan.",
             "At a work station it was intended to empty a circuit to inspect. The intervention would have facilitated the diagnosis, but returned water to the common tank and changed the balance just at the time they were trying to observe. Lucia first suggested isolate the section and check what reading was still moving. The essay saved minutes and left more information for review.\nWhen they re-built the procedure, they added at every step the reason to do so in that order. For whoever came quickly, the result of his passing a reading was as clear as his name."},
            {"The Handover",
             "The last practice admitted several overlapping judgements. A reading was late to come and a person had been changing a slogan and some of the registration had bad hours. The team first rebuilt the tested changes, before searching for a cause for every drawing on the screen. When she found an interval without data, she put it blank. It was best if someone could recognize the hole to have a calculation cover up.\nAt the end, the simulator returned to a stable state. The assessment focused on the minutes that had passed with uncertainty and the decisions that they could still get rid of.",
             "Lucia prepared a sheet for the next shift. It included the current status of deposits, outstanding measurements, who had confirmed every change and a reason to call back to the previous team. They had achieved a system with better indicators. They had also obtained a handover that someone tired could understand without re-reading the whole practice.\nBefore shutting down the simulator, Lucia reviewed last hour consumption and wrote it at the margin. It was a regular figure that filled the balance sheet. The incoming team had from her a point from which to start to doubt."}
        }}},
        {"Mapping the Smoke","Dissemination","A narrative essay about what an orbital image allows knowing during a fire.","#66543b",{{
            {"The First Image",
             "The map of a fire begins with a number of choices. Someone decides what an image serves as a reference, how long to separate the shots and what colors help to distinguish smoke, soil and vegetation. Any of these decisions change their reading. Bruno gathered three images from the same valley and shown them to a brigade. The most striking had a black spot with firm edges. The others saw that much of that stain belonged to a shadow slope.\nIn the next version, the time of each photo was next to the scale.",
             "The brigade used another map where important lines were roads, access to deposits and areas that could be isolated. The satellite's image added a sign about the fire but a dark pixel told little about the possibility of crossing a stream. Bruno asked them to mark the questions that the map had to answer. A secure path required calling whoever could see the stretch from ground. A fire extension estimate needed to compare images taken under similar conditions.\nThe questions were written by the map and changed as the afternoon advanced."},
            {"Seeing Through Time",
             "Putting two images together invites you to look for changes. Comparison requires matching and monitoring of the same surfaces. A path can move onto the screen because the angle of observation has been changed. A spot can be clarified because the air's cleaner or because the instrument's registered another band. At the table at his place, Bruno had his photo pair alternate quickly. The movement of stable edges revealed registration errors.\nThey used to be put first into a river curve and onto the reservoir's contour.",
             "A heat source could appear even if the visible photo was covered with smoke. The team compared the different layers before drawing the front. He knew that the measurements had different boundaries: one indicated relative temperature, another allowed for ground recognition and another was taken with a different frequency. The value of crossing them was with the questions that they solved together.\nOn a hill, a spot was held two nights at the same place. A local report identified an installation that emits heat regularly. From that date onwards, it was marked as a reference so as to avoid confusing her with a new focus."},
            {"The line and its gaps",
             "The afternoon of the second day was an information-free stretch. On the printed map, the system automatically united the ends of the estimated route. The straight line crossed a breakage that nobody had confirmed. Bruno replaced that segment with an area marked as uncertain and made clear the hour of observations at both ends. The brigade immediately found out what part of the drawing she could check from the ground.\nThe change also forced the team to recognize that an advanced presentation could transmit a security that the registry did not contain.",
             "Two versions with different tasks were used to communicate the map. A synthesis allowed an understanding of what roads had been established and at what time. The work version retained gangs, comparisons and values that signified something only within that analysis. Bruno put the same reference for both of them. Thus, when a person phone with doubt about a path, they could both place the same portion.\nThe file included missed photos with a brief note about the cause. A few months later they served to review why the initial contour had been so different."},
            {"After the emergency",
             "The first rain cleaned up some of the smoke. A few days later, the beak picked up ashes and sediments from the slopes. Some burned zones began to change color well before a stable vegetable cover returned. The following month's images performed another function: they shown locations where it was worth returning with a field team. Bruno compared with photographs prior to the fire and used local tours to select observation points.\nThe annual map would have slower changes and require monitoring of the seasonal diversity of the valley as well.",
             "At the winter review, a brigade member recognized a path that the emergency map had left without name. They went about them to measure their distance and note their difficulties. During that fire it had been useful to quickly understand what an image had been saying about the valley. They were later allowed to give back information that the satellite did not distinguish from the map.\nBruno saved his photographs and noted how they had made every decision. The following season his team would have better reference, along with a record of how he had learned how to read it."}
        }}},
        {"A night at the terminator","Mission Chronicle","A fictitious lunar expedition prepares a journey to the edge of the shade.","#55405d",{{
            {"The path under the Sun",
             "When Amira received the first map, the path seemed unambitious. The equipment would be rolling for 6 hours and would stop about a highlight that the photographs of the orbit clearly shown. The expedition had chosen that point with low light, which allowed to distinguish small irregularities from the ground. Instead, the rocks leave long shadows that confuse the distances.\nDuring the meeting, Amira requested to mark the locations where the car could reverse the march. The path back deserved the same attention but, in drawing, followed an already drawn line.",
             "The journey was starting with a wide ground. A half way up between two depressions. The vehicle's camera offered a useful frame if they advanced slowly and held a fixed point ahead. The tests put a row of signals to mark how much visible ground they needed to turn. The repetition shown that they could conduct the manoeuvre with less space than they had reserved. Even so, they have preserved their space for the actual ground.\nThe test distance, the plan distance and the reason for the difference remained with the log. No one would have to rebuild that argument while travelling."},
            {"The Signal on the Ridge",
             "At second hour, coverage began to vary. Two antennas remained available and the relief hidden one as the vehicle went down a few feet down the slopes. Amira had been planning to get his link back as she approached a flat rock, but the mark she had used on the map was unrecognizable as she saw it closely. The photo of the orbit seemed separate from the others. From the ground was part of a group of blocks.\nThey stopped and sent their position through the slower channel and compared their shadows with a photo taken before they came out.",
             "The link came back during a pause. They used them to transmit a single photo, showing how far they could turn. The rest of the data remained in the vehicle. The expedition had time to tidy up if I found another place to have contact with. Amira requested that the support team first seek stop sites and leave the monitoring tasks for later.\nHe wanted to know how far back I had to go if his path closed later. The group found two alternatives and was able to prepare an order to prove them."},
            {"The First Shadow",
             "The edge of the shade crossed the path earlier than intended. The camera adjusted the exposure and for a few seconds only shown a bright cut at the horizon. Amira waited to get the ground back on the screen. The vehicle was leveled and could be stopped again later, but the shade was between two rock banks. He thought about the evidence of the manoeuvre and looked back at the width of his exit.\nOn the map, that bench belonged to a mark that up to that time seemed to be an image defect.",
             "The decision to change the path was noted with three photographs and an approximate shadow position. They have taken the highest alternative. They earned light surface to distinguish the edges and regained the sign from the repeater. The ascent consumed some of the reserve intended for measurements. Amira cancelled one of the stops and preserved the sample they could collect with the car still.\nThe result was shorter and leave a photo that the team could use to get back to the place. The notation described the size of the shade and the instant they had seen it."},
            {"The Return",
             "Upon his return, the shadow of the blocks had been changed. The team once again used extensive brands, including an edge and a long crest curve. They had been saving energy to repeat some of their journey if they were lost. They only needed to do so once, at a bifurcation where a brand was covered with light change. The image of his return completed his missing record at his exit.\nAt their meeting the following day they dedicated several minutes to that issue. The orbital map remained correct. What they had adjusted was his way of leading with him.",
             "A few months later, the relief team regained his sample and repeated a portion of his journey. Amira had sent them back and forth photos with the time and direction of each take. When they reached the ridge, they compared them with the state of the ground and confirmed their path in a single attempt.\nThe last line of the log was put on the back page. He indicated what brand they would first see and from where they would have space to turn. To whoever happened there next time, that fact would give something concrete to check as the shade approached."}
        }}}
    }};
    return collection;
}
